1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,710
From November 1942 to May 1943, the Second World War was fought across Tunisia.

2
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:14,080
An American reporter wrote about it in a tent. A British tank officer turned it into poems.

3
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:21,900
In occupied Tunis, a Jewish lawyer kept a diary. A young man from the city's Jewish quarter later made it a novel.

4
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:28,550
Four writers, four places to stand. This is the war in Tunisia, in words.

5
00:00:34,550 --> 00:00:40,870
In 1942, Tunisia was a French protectorate, ruled in name by the Bey of Tunis.

6
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:49,020
On 8 November, the Allies landed in Morocco and Algeria. The next day, German aircraft landed at Tunis.

7
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:56,740
German and Italian troops held Tunis, Bizerte and the east, as Allied armies closed in from Algeria and Libya.

8
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:03,430
Tunisians of every faith, Muslims, Jews and Christians, lived through the occupation and the fighting.

9
00:01:03,510 --> 00:01:14,100
On 7 May 1943, Allied troops entered Tunis and Bizerte. On 13 May, the last Axis armies in Africa surrendered.

10
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:17,900
Our four writers each saw a different part of those six months.

11
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:24,720
Ernie Pyle followed American soldiers on the western front, from the central plains to the northern hills.

12
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:30,570
Keith Douglas fought with British tanks from Egypt across Libya, and reached Tunisia in the spring.

13
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:36,720
Paul Ghez lived the occupation inside Tunis, as one of the leaders of its Jewish community.

14
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:44,640
And Albert Memmi, a young man from Tunis's Jewish quarter, saw it from the city, then from a labour camp.

15
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:53,640
Two of their books came out in 1943, while the war went on, one in 1946, and one in 1953.

16
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:59,910
For each, we'll ask who wrote it, what kind of book it is, and what is fact or fiction.

17
00:02:03,310 --> 00:02:12,650
Ernie Pyle, a Scripps-Howard newspaper columnist, came to North Africa in late 1942, and lived as the American soldiers did.

18
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:20,670
In February 1943, he retreated with them from Sbeitla. In March, he was at Gafsa, retaken in the south.

19
00:02:20,750 --> 00:02:28,320
In April, he joined the First Infantry Division in the hills before Mateur, and in May, he reached Bizerte.

20
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:36,490
This is reportage: short columns written within days, collected in 1943 as a book, Here Is Your War.

21
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:45,060
His column ran in 42 newspapers in November 1942, and in 122 by April 1943.

22
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:51,430
He left the generals' view to others. In his words: "I only know what we see from our worm's-eye view."

23
00:02:51,510 --> 00:02:59,050
That view was tired men, mud, fear and graves, with the soldiers named, and their home towns, often their streets.

24
00:02:59,130 --> 00:03:06,470
It is reporting, not fiction. But it shows only what one American saw, and what the army's censors let through.

25
00:03:06,550 --> 00:03:15,720
Tunisians stay at its edges, often seen through French officers' prejudices, though he noted that both armies cleared Arab families from the fighting.

26
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:23,940
He won the Pulitzer Prize in 1944, and was killed in the Pacific in April 1945.

27
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:33,230
Keith Douglas was a British poet and, at 22, a tank officer in the Egyptian desert.

28
00:03:33,310 --> 00:03:40,300
At El Alamein in October 1942, he left a staff job and went to his regiment without orders.

29
00:03:40,380 --> 00:03:49,220
With the Sherwood Rangers Yeomanry he fought across Libya, until a mine wounded him at Wadi Zem Zem in January 1943.

30
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:57,370
In hospital he began the war poems that made his name, then that spring rejoined his regiment, now in Tunisia.

31
00:03:57,450 --> 00:04:04,520
His prose book, Alamein to Zem Zem, is a memoir of his months in the desert, up to the day he was wounded.

32
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:11,540
He illustrated it with his own drawings. It came out in 1946, two years after his death.

33
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:19,940
A memoir tells real events, as its writer remembers and chooses them. His colonel, Edward Kellett, appears under a nickname.

34
00:04:20,020 --> 00:04:28,510
His poems go further. In Vergissmeinnicht, German for forget me not, soldiers return to a battlefield three weeks after a fight.

35
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:35,380
They find the German gunner who had hit the narrator's tank, and a photograph signed by his girlfriend, Steffi.

36
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:41,230
Douglas wrote: "For here the lover and killer are mingled." The poem sees both in one man.

37
00:04:41,310 --> 00:04:51,530
He went back to England in December 1943, and was killed in Normandy on 9 June 1944, aged 24.

38
00:04:54,930 --> 00:05:02,500
Paul Ghez, born in Sousse in 1898, was a lawyer in Tunis and a member of its Jewish community council.

39
00:05:02,580 --> 00:05:12,100
With the German troops came an SS unit under Walter Rauff. Tunisia's Jews faced fines, confiscations, hostage-taking and forced labour.

40
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:20,720
In December, the SS demanded Jewish workers. The community's leaders had to form a committee to recruit them, headed by Ghez.

41
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:27,540
His book is a diary, in dated entries, from November 1942 to May 1943.

42
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:34,110
He called it Six mois sous la botte, Six months under the boot. It was printed in Tunis in 1943.

43
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:42,150
It records the orders and the lists. By 14 December, he counts 3,585 men recruited.

44
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:47,270
Some marched to a camp at Cheylus. Others worked at airfields, or near the front.

45
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:52,690
Families came to his office to ask for their sons, and he tried to bring the numbers down.

46
00:05:52,770 --> 00:06:01,610
In all, about 5,000 Jewish men were forced to work, in some forty camps, by the US Holocaust Memorial Museum's estimate.

47
00:06:01,690 --> 00:06:10,980
On 7 May, as the Allies entered Tunis, he arranged the workers' return. The next day, he found his flat emptied.

48
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:18,670
Nothing in it is invented. But it is one man's view, from a terrible position between German orders and the families.

49
00:06:18,750 --> 00:06:26,820
Other accounts of those months exist, and the historian Haïm Saadoun notes that they do not always agree.

50
00:06:30,020 --> 00:06:36,840
Albert Memmi was born in 1920 by the Hara, the old Jewish quarter of Tunis, a saddler's son.

51
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:42,830
At home he spoke the Arabic of Tunis's Jews. A scholarship took him to the French lycée.

52
00:06:42,910 --> 00:06:51,250
In 1942–43, he went to a forced labour camp, more or less of his own free will, as he later said.

53
00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:58,320
In 1953, he published his first novel, La Statue de sel, The Pillar of Salt, with a preface by Albert Camus.

54
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:04,010
It is a novel. Its narrator has another name, Alexandre Mordekhaï Benillouche.

55
00:07:04,090 --> 00:07:11,130
Alexandre grows up in an alley by the Hara, goes to the French lycée, and feels a stranger in his own city.

56
00:07:11,210 --> 00:07:16,050
Vichy France's anti-Jewish laws tell him he will never be fully French.

57
00:07:16,130 --> 00:07:23,240
When the Germans demand Jewish workers, Memmi has him say: "I asked to go because it was intolerable to stay."

58
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:28,240
In the camp, Alexandre keeps a diary, and later escapes with friends.

59
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:35,660
Much of this is Memmi's own life. The historian Haïm Saadoun argues that the novel rests on his camp diary.

60
00:07:35,740 --> 00:07:46,060
The ending is invented: Alexandre sails for South America. Memmi studied in Algiers and Paris, taught in Tunis, then settled in France.

61
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:53,910
The title recalls Lot's wife in Genesis, turned to salt for looking back. A novel looks back too.

62
00:07:57,510 --> 00:08:01,480
Lay the four books side by side, across the same six months.

63
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:07,350
Pyle saw the front from the American side. Douglas saw the desert war from a British tank.

64
00:08:07,430 --> 00:08:13,540
Ghez saw what soldiers never saw: the occupation, from inside the Jewish community of Tunis.

65
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:19,710
Memmi lived it as a young Tunisian Jew, and ten years later reshaped it as fiction.

66
00:08:19,790 --> 00:08:26,750
Reportage is fast and close, but censored. A diary is exact about dates, but sees from one desk.

67
00:08:26,830 --> 00:08:32,270
A memoir chooses what to remember. A novel invents, to show what a life felt like.

68
00:08:32,350 --> 00:08:41,590
Four men's books still tell little of the Muslim families whose fields became battlefields, the women of Tunis, or Italian and German soldiers.

69
00:08:41,670 --> 00:08:48,210
No single book is the whole war. Read together, and with care, they show far more of it.

70
00:08:48,290 --> 00:08:55,210
Ask of every book who wrote it, from where, and when, and the war in Tunisia comes closer.
